// **A determinate herb: one erect axis that stops, and an organ that stops
// it.** The organ package's first authored habit, and the plant this engine
// could not express before it -- every axis here has been indeterminate since
// the substrate was written, which is a large part of why every species is a
// variation on one plant.
//
// **Not a named real plant, and the distinction is on the record.**
// `Reports/plant-morphology-evolvability-2026-08-26.md` §6 says it as a
// heading -- *"The acceptance artifact is not a sunflower ... Twelve tomatoes
// = it does not [work]"*. The older `plant-morphology-reach-2026-08-23.md` §6
// does name one as the acceptance artifact; the evolvability report supersedes
// it on that point, and this comment is here because the first draft of this
// file followed the superseded one.
//
// **What the existing set has in common is one thing only, and it is not a
// taxonomy.** `tree`, `conifer`, `shrub`, `creeper`, `grass`, `moss` are a
// mixture: two are growth forms (`shrub`, `creeper`), three are vernacular
// clade names (`conifer`, `grass`, `moss`), and `tree` is a growth form that
// half the others also satisfy -- a conifer *is* a tree, so the set is not
// even mutually exclusive. What every one of them is, is a **category** of
// plant rather than a particular plant. That is the whole of the rule, and
// it is the reason a species-level name is wrong here: it commits the file
// to matching one real organism, which this engine cannot do and should not
// try to.
//
// **And note where a sunflower or a tomato would have fitted: nowhere.**
// There is no herbaceous non-grass slot in that list at all. The gap is the
// honest argument for a new file, and a better one than the naming rule.
//
// Everything structural here is data that already existed. What is new is
// three values in the fate table (`becomes: Flower`, `after_metamers`, and
// the two `Ripe` rules), three materials, and a price. There is no new growth
// engine: `Reports/plant-morphology-reach-2026-08-23.md` §1's whole answer is
// that the random walk is the *variation* mechanism and what a determinate
// herb needs from it is **identity** -- an axis that knows when to stop and
// an organ to stop in.
//
// **Why this shape, against the four woody species.** The owner's standing
// complaint is that the species differ in size and colour and nothing else,
// and the diagnosis (`Reports/plant-appearance-design.md`) is that every
// architectural lever tried so far relabels a cell without moving a
// silhouette that texture and composition set. So the levers here are the
// ones that move composition outright:
//
//   - `max_active_tips: 2` with `branch_chance: 0`, so there is one leader
//     and not a crown (the cap counts the asking tip, so 1 means no growth);
//   - `plastochron: [5]` with `leaf_cluster: 8`, so the leaves are large,
//     widely spaced, and leave bare stem between them;
//   - `after_metamers: 8`, so the axis **stops** rather than filling the
//     sky, which is the thing no species in this engine has ever done;
//   - and a 9-cell flower head in a palette with no green in it.
//
// A stalk with a flower head on it is not a smaller tree.
//
// **The turgor bound and the metamer count have to be set together, and the
// first pass got it wrong in a way worth recording.** `turgor_source: 0.32`
// with `turgor_per_cell: 0.006` puts the height ceiling at
// `(0.32 - 0.1) / 0.006 = 36` cells of path length, and 9 metamers at a
// plastochron of 4 needs 36 -- so the axis reached its ceiling exactly as it
// reached its count, and the taper had been slowing it for a long way before
// that. Measured: **9 axes terminated across 32 established plants**, and a
// contact sheet of six showed one flower. The symptom is the one the
// determinacy trap warns about -- a low counter with no visible cause -- and
// the cause was not the fate table at all. 0.46 puts the ceiling at 60
// against a terminal at 40, which is the margin this needs.
(
    name: "herb",
    // Yellow-green foliage, noticeably lighter than a tree's -- which
    // separates this from `tree` (bands 2-3) and `shrub` (4-5) at the same
    // time as the flower does.
    foliage_bands: (first: 3, count: 2),
    // Pale green stem, not bark: a herbaceous axis is not a trunk. Bands
    // 6-7 of `wood` are the lightest it has.
    bark_bands: (first: 6, count: 2),
    // **Yellow and orange, bands 0-1 of `flower`.** Deliberately the two
    // furthest from anything foliage can be.
    flower_bands: (first: 0, count: 2),
    // Gold and orange fruit — a ripe seed head, not a berry.
    fruit_bands: (first: 4, count: 2),
    windfall_material: "windfall",
    stomatal_reserve: 0.2,
    // A ruderal's bank: short-lived seed, fast-rotting remains. Both an
    // order of magnitude below `tree`'s, which is what a herb *is* on the
    // r/K axis -- see `SpeciesDef::seed_half_life`.
    seed_half_life: 14000.0,
    remains_half_life: 700.0,
    fates: [
        (GrowingTip, [
            // **The determinate terminal, and it is listed first because
            // first match wins.** Nine metamers up, the apex is consumed by
            // a flower: no `child`, no `lateral`, no continuation of any
            // kind. That is what determinate means, and it is a `becomes`
            // mutation -- the field the viability gate took 34 mutations on
            // without producing one sterile plant, while `child` killed 5
            // of 6 (`Reports/plant-fate-viability-2026-08-28.md` §2).
            (when: Node,  becomes: Flower, after_metamers: Some(8)),
            // Until then, an ordinary shoot. Identical to `tree`'s rules --
            // an axis is an axis; the only thing this species changes is
            // when it ends.
            (when: Node,  becomes: DormantBud, child: Some(GrowingTip), lateral: Some(GrowingTip)),
            (when: Grew,  becomes: MatureBody, child: Some(GrowingTip), lateral: Some(GrowingTip)),
            // **Aged out with nowhere to go, and it does NOT flower here.**
            // A tip that starved short of nine metamers has not earned a
            // head, and giving it one would make the metamer count
            // decorative: every axis would flower eventually, whatever its
            // `after_metamers` said. A stalk with no head is what this
            // plant in deep shade should look like.
            (when: Stale, becomes: MatureBody),
        ]),
        (RootTip, [
            (when: Grew,  becomes: MatureBody, child: Some(RootTip), lateral: Some(RootTip)),
            (when: Stale, becomes: MatureBody),
        ]),
        (DormantBud, [
            (when: Flush, becomes: GrowingTip),
        ]),
        // **The organ sequence, as two rules.** A flower that has been open
        // long enough sets; a fruit that has finished ripening lets go and
        // falls carrying the seed. Both fire from `Behavior::Ripen`'s clock.
        (Flower, [
            (when: Ripe, becomes: Fruit),
        ]),
        (Fruit, [
            // `becomes: Seed` is the one fate the engine resolves by
            // *detaching* rather than relabelling -- see `plant::drop_organ`.
            (when: Ripe, becomes: Seed),
        ]),
    ],
    cell_types: [
        (Seed, [
            Germinate(
                light_threshold: 0.1,
                // An annual germinates readily -- it is betting on one
                // season, not funding a trunk.
                soil_water_threshold: 0.15,
                instant: false,
            ),
        ]),
        (GrowingTip, [
            Grow(
                cost: 0.15,
                // **Near zero: one stalk.** Not literally zero, because a
                // plant that cannot branch at all has no way to recover
                // from losing its apex, and because a rare basal side-shoot
                // is what a real erect herb does under damage.
                branch_chance: [0.0, 0.0],
                continuation_weight: 0.95,
                light_weight: [0.06, 0.06],
                // Almost no wind lean: a stalk stands up.
                wind_weight: 0.03,
                // Hard up. This is a leader, not a crown.
                upward_weight: [0.98, 0.9],
                crowding_weight: 30.0,
                // **2, and it may not be 1.** `plant.rs`'s cap gate reads
                // `active_tip_count >= max_active_tips` and the tip asking
                // is itself counted, so 1 means *no growth at all*: measured,
                // a whole stand germinated, staled at one cell, went
                // senescent for want of a vital cell and rotted away inside
                // 8,000 frames -- population 0, which reads exactly like a
                // broken species file. With `branch_chance: 0` beside it, 2
                // is one leader and nothing else.
                max_active_tips: 2,
                // A node every four cells, so nine metamers is a stalk of
                // roughly 36 cells before the head.
                plastochron: [5, 5],
                penetration_force: 0.0,
                // Herbaceous turgor: a shorter reach than a tree's, which is
                // the height half of "not a tree".
                turgor_source: 0.46,
                turgor_yield: 0.1,
                turgor_per_cell: 0.006,
                turgor_taper: 0.35,
                // Very stiff: the stalk barely wanders.
                heading_inertia: 0.93,
                branch_angle: [40.0, 35.0],
                internode: [3, 3],
                // **Big leaves, widely spaced.** The composition lever:
                // this is a few large blades on a bare stem, where a tree is
                // a continuous canopy of small ones.
                leaf_cluster: 8,
                // **The head.** Nine cells is roughly three times the
                // width of the stalk bearing it, which is the size half of
                // the only lever that has ever read here -- see
                // `Behavior::Grow::organ_cluster`.
                organ_cluster: 9,
                juvenile_size: 8,
                juvenile_plastochron: 0.6,
                juvenile_branch: 1.0,
                genotype_variance: (0.4, 0.0, 0.35, 0.18, 0.6, 0.0, 0.4, 0.5, 0.0, 0.7),
                sympodial: [false],
                tropism: [Orthotropic],
            ),
            Photosynthesize(rate: 0.55, shade_death: 0.0, drought_death: 0.0),
        ]),
        (RootTip, [
            Absorb(rate: 1.6),
            Grow(
                cost: 0.18,
                branch_chance: [0.35],
                continuation_weight: 0.65,
                light_weight: [0.0],
                wind_weight: 0.0,
                upward_weight: [0.35],
                crowding_weight: 0.0,
                max_active_tips: 10,
                plastochron: [0],
                heading_inertia: 0.5,
                penetration_force: 1.0,
                genotype_variance: (0.0, 0.5, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.4, 0.0, 0.0, 0.25, 0.0),
            ),
        ]),
        (MatureBody, [
            // **The mature-cell seed path stays**, per the owner's call of
            // 2026-08-28: organs are an *additional* route to a seed, not a
            // replacement. A plant that never manages a head can still
            // reproduce, slowly, the way every other plant here does.
            //
            // `seed_maturity: 60` against `shrub`'s 250: a herb breeds small.
            // And organs do not count toward it -- see
            // `OrganismState::organ_cells`.
            Reproduce(seed_cost: 0.18, reproductive_allocation: 0.28, seed_maturity: 60),
            // A stalk thickens a little; it is not a trunk. High
            // `pipe_ratio` means many nodes of foliage per cell of width.
            SecondaryThicken(pipe_ratio: 14.0),
            StructuralAnchor,
            Absorb(rate: 1.6),
        ]),
        (DormantBud, [
            BudBreak(
                cost: 0.15,
                thickening_survival: 0.5,
                // Strongly acrotonous: what breaks is near the top, which
                // keeps the plant a leader rather than a bush.
                acrotony: 1.8,
            ),
            StructuralAnchor,
            SecondaryThicken(pipe_ratio: 14.0),
        ]),
        (Leaf, [
            Photosynthesize(rate: 0.45, shade_death: 0.0008, drought_death: 0.0008),
        ]),
        // **The organ cell types, and both are inert but for the clock.**
        // No `Photosynthesize`, no `Grow`, no `StructuralAnchor`: an organ
        // holds up nothing and earns nothing, which is exactly what makes
        // building one a real cost rather than an investment that pays for
        // itself.
        (Flower, [
            // ~50 organism ticks open before it sets. Slow enough that a
            // stand is visibly *in flower* for a long stretch rather than
            // flickering through it, which matters when the artifact is a
            // contact sheet.
            Ripen(rate: 0.02, cost: 0.3),
        ]),
        (Fruit, [
            // Slower again: a seed head takes longer to fill than a flower
            // takes to set. `cost` is the provisioning that rides down with
            // the seed as its `endowment`.
            Ripen(rate: 0.012, cost: 0.35),
        ]),
    ],
)
